The Ardia Linux host draws the whole window itself: its sidebar and page card sit on the BrowserView, and it hides the
caption buttons. Chromium's custom Linux frame still wrapped that view in a themed band with a resize and shadow border,
a rounded clip and a window-coloured frame, so on a compositor that draws no title bar (Hyprland) every Ardia window had
a thick border with its own corners outside the sidebar and the page card. The host names its windows through
g_ardia_linux_borderless_frame; for those windows the frame view and its layout report no border, input or shadow
insets and no corner radius, and paint no frame, so the client area is the whole window. A thin band along the edges
still answers as a resize border so a window can be resized where the compositor draws none. Settings and Import
(g_ardia_linux_frameless_app) count as hosted too. The hook is null outside the Linux host.
Chrome maps its startup window before the host starts, so X11 sets _GTK_FRAME_EXTENTS from the frame insets it had
then and rewrites it only on a window state change. When the host lays the frame out again (FrameTypeChanged), the
browser's window tree host now asks X11 for the insets once more through X11Extension::RefreshDecorationInsets, so
a window manager that honours the property sees none. Wayland has no X11 extension and is unchanged.
